<root BTCPP_format="4">
    <!-- Two separate loops, deliberately not conflated:
         - KeepRunningUntilFailure plus Inverter(CheckGameOver) is the GAME loop: it
           plays moves until a king is captured (or a fault forces the tree to give
           up). This is the loop straight_tree.xml's RunUntilSuccess used to run by
           overloading a per-move condition failure as "loop again"; doing that
           here would make RecoveryNode treat every ordinary non-final move as a
           fault (see git history for the regression this replaces).
         - RecoveryNode handles genuine faults only. Its work branch performs one
           full move; the recovery branch always releases the piece and lifts clear
           before the work branch is allowed to run again. A move that succeeds
           never touches the recovery branch at all.

         A paused system (the E-stop path: lifecycle_manager PAUSE deactivates
         behavior_server) is a third instance of the same shape as waiting for the
         opponent below: a normal, expected non-progress state, not a fault. For
         that reason IsSystemActive is NOT the first element of RecoveryNode's work
         branch; instead it gates entry to RecoveryNode itself, via the
         PlayMoveOrWaitForSystemActive fallback. behavior_server is precisely the
         node that PAUSE deactivates, so if IsSystemActive failed *inside* the work
         branch, RecoveryNode would treat the pause as a fault and run its recovery
         subtree, which also needs behavior_server and also fails, burning all
         three retries and ending KeepRunningUntilFailure with no tree left for a
         RESUME to act on. Gating RecoveryNode's entry instead means a paused tick
         issues no arm goal, spends no retry, and the game loop keeps spinning so
         play resumes on its own once the system is reactivated.

         PlayMoveOrWaitForSystemActive MUST be a ReactiveFallback, not a plain
         Fallback. A plain Fallback remembers which child last returned RUNNING
         and, once RecoveryNode starts a move, never re-ticks IsSystemActive again
         until that move finishes on its own (see FallbackNode::tick() in
         BehaviorTree.CPP: current_child_idx_ is only advanced on FAILURE or
         SUCCESS, never reset while a child keeps returning RUNNING). A PAUSE
         arriving mid-move would then be invisible until the move either finishes
         or the in-flight ArmActionClient times out against the now-deactivated
         behavior_server and fails its way through RecoveryNode's retries, exactly
         the failure this guard exists to prevent, reached through a still-running
         cycle instead of a cycle boundary. ReactiveFallback re-ticks every child
         from the top on every single BT tick, so it notices the moment
         IsSystemActive starts failing and halts the running RecoveryNode branch
         immediately, mid-move.

         Known consequence of halting mid-move: the arm may still be holding a
         piece when a PAUSE lands. RESUME restarts the work branch from the top of
         PlayOneMove, whose first action drives the arm to home_position with
         hand_close_position still set, i.e. while still gripping, which drags the
         held piece across the board. Releasing before retreating is exactly the
         hazard RecoverArm's step order exists to avoid; wiring an equivalent
         release into the resume path is a real design change, out of scope for
         this fix. It is accepted deliberately here: behavior_server is already
         deactivated by the time a mid-move halt happens, so no motion occurs
         either way, and an arm that keeps moving after an E-stop is strictly
         worse than one frozen holding a piece. -->
    <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
        <KeepRunningUntilFailure>
            <Sequence name="PlayUntilGameOver">

                <!-- IsSystemActive SUCCEEDs only while behavior_server is ACTIVE, so
                     Inverter(IsSystemActive) SUCCEEDs exactly while the system is
                     paused, short-circuiting this fallback before RecoveryNode is
                     ever ticked. When the system is active, Inverter FAILs and the
                     fallback falls through to the real work below. This must stay
                     a ReactiveFallback (see the header comment) so a PAUSE landing
                     mid-move halts RecoveryNode immediately instead of only being
                     noticed at the next move boundary. -->
                <ReactiveFallback name="PlayMoveOrWaitForSystemActive">
                    <Inverter>
                        <IsSystemActive lifecycle_manager_name="lifecycle_manager" />
                    </Inverter>

                    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="3">

                        <Sequence name="PlayOneMove">
                            <Script code="home_position := '-1.5807;0.0;1.5807;0.0'; hand_open_position := '0.20;0.20;0.20'; hand_close_position := '0.04;0.04;0.04'" />
                            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}"
                                target_joint_positions="{home_position}"
                                target_hand_position="{hand_close_position}" />
                            <BoardActionIsaacClient server_name="board_action" sec="15" nanosec="0"
                                player_side="{player_side}" fen="{fen}" />
                            <MakeDecisionClient player_side="{player_side}" fen="{fen}"
                                hand_open_position="{hand_open_position}"
                                hand_close_position="{hand_close_position}"
                                move_type="{move_type}"
                                move_count="{move_count}"
                                move_from1="{move_from1}" move_from_down1="{move_from_down1}"
                                move_to1="{move_to1}" move_to_down1="{move_to_down1}"
                                move_from2="{move_from2}" move_from_down2="{move_from_down2}"
                                move_to2="{move_to2}" move_to_down2="{move_to_down2}"
                                move_from3="{move_from3}" move_from_down3="{move_from_down3}"
                                move_to3="{move_to3}" move_to_down3="{move_to_down3}"
                                box_from1="{box_from1}" box_to1="{box_to1}"
                                box_from2="{box_from2}" box_to2="{box_to2}"
                                box_from3="{box_from3}" box_to3="{box_to3}" />
                            <!-- Waiting for the opponent is not a fault. MakeDecisionClient
                                 reports it as SUCCESS with move_type "wait", and this guard
                                 skips the move subtree so no retry is spent and no recovery
                                 runs. Without the guard, Foreach would tick with move_count 0
                                 and FAIL (foreach_node.cpp treats count <= 0 as failure),
                                 which is exactly the fault we are avoiding. The arm is
                                 already at home from the top of this sequence, so a waiting
                                 tick plans no motion at all. -->
                            <Fallback name="MoveOrWaitForOpponent">
                                <ConditionNode name="CheckWaitingForOpponent"
                                    param1="{move_type}" param2="wait" />
                                <Sequence name="ExecuteMove">
                                    <Foreach name="MoveLoop"
                                        move_count="{move_count}"
                                        move_from1="{move_from1}" move_from_down1="{move_from_down1}"
                                        move_to1="{move_to1}" move_to_down1="{move_to_down1}"
                                        move_from2="{move_from2}" move_from_down2="{move_from_down2}"
                                        move_to2="{move_to2}" move_to_down2="{move_to_down2}"
                                        move_from3="{move_from3}" move_from_down3="{move_from_down3}"
                                        move_to3="{move_to3}" move_to_down3="{move_to_down3}"
                                        box_from1="{box_from1}" box_to1="{box_to1}"
                                        box_from2="{box_from2}" box_to2="{box_to2}"
                                        box_from3="{box_from3}" box_to3="{box_to3}"
                                        move_from="{move_from}" move_from_down="{move_from_down}"
                                        move_to="{move_to}" move_to_down="{move_to_down}"
                                        box_from="{box_from}" box_to="{box_to}">
                                        <SubTree ID="MoveSequence" _autoremap="true"/>
                                    </Foreach>
                                    <!-- No target_square: this call's own goal and its start
                                         state (wherever the last MoveSequence step left the
                                         arm) are both clear of every square's cylinder, so
                                         nothing needs removing here. board_fen is still passed
                                         so this retreat plans against a defined snapshot of the
                                         board rather than whatever the last MoveSequence step's
                                         target_square-driven removal happened to leave behind;
                                         see Important 2 in the Task 7 review. -->
                                    <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}"
                                        target_joint_positions="{home_position}"
                                        target_hand_position="{hand_close_position}"
                                        board_fen="{fen}" />
                                </Sequence>
                            </Fallback>
                        </Sequence>

                        <SubTree ID="RecoverArm" _autoremap="true"/>

                    </RecoveryNode>
                </ReactiveFallback>

                <Inverter>
                    <ConditionNode name="CheckGameOver" param1="{move_type}" param2="killking" />
                </Inverter>

            </Sequence>
        </KeepRunningUntilFailure>
    </BehaviorTree>

    <!-- Order matters and is the whole point: open the gripper BEFORE moving,
         so a piece held at the moment of failure is set down where it is
         rather than dragged across the board.

         board_fen is passed on every goal here (no target_square: none of
         these three goals descends onto a specific square) so the recovery
         plans against a defined snapshot of the board rather than whatever a
         fault mid-MoveSequence happened to leave in the scene, e.g. a
         target_square removal from the step that was running when the fault
         hit, never restored because the fault ended the sequence before its
         later steps (which would have re-added it, or moved target_square on
         to a different square) ran. See Important 2 in the Task 7 review. -->
    <BehaviorTree ID="RecoverArm">
        <Sequence name="RecoverArm">
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}" hand_only="true"
                target_hand_position="{hand_open_position}" board_fen="{fen}" />
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}"
                target_joint_positions="{recovery_lift_position}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_open_position}" board_fen="{fen}" />
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}"
                target_joint_positions="{home_position}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_close_position}" board_fen="{fen}" />
        </Sequence>
    </BehaviorTree>

    <!-- board_fen refreshes the planning scene's collision objects before every
         goal (Task 7 / F6). target_square is set on every call whose *current*
         joint state, not just its goal, sits at or inside the square the
         object being removed represents, because MoveIt validates the start
         state for collision, not only the goal:
           - steps 2-3 (move_from_down) and steps 6-7 (move_to_down): the
             obvious case, both calls' own goal is the down position.
           - step 4 (move_from, hand closed): its START state is still
             move_from_down (step 2/3 left the arm there) and it is carrying
             the piece just grasped from box_from, so box_from's collision
             object must stay out through this call too, or the rebuild would
             re-add a cylinder exactly where the arm already is and MoveIt
             would report "start state in collision" before it even plans.
           - step 8 (move_to, hand open): its START state is still
             move_to_down (step 6/7 left it there), sitting on whatever the
             rebuild just put/removed at box_to (a real object for a capture,
             since box_to was occupied in the pre-move board_fen). Same
             failure mode as step 4 if target_square is missing here.
         The piece is in the gripper for the entire span from step 4 through
         step 8, so box_from's/box_to's cylinder should not exist during any
         call across that span; steps 5-6-7 already get this for free because
         their own target_square (box_to on 6-7) or the "up" clearance height
         (step 5) keeps them out of trouble, but 4 and 8 need it stated
         explicitly since they are the ones whose *start* state, not goal,
         is the risk. -->
    <BehaviorTree ID="MoveSequence">
        <Sequence name="MoveSequence">
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}" target_joint_positions="{move_from}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_open_position}" board_fen="{fen}" />
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}" target_joint_positions="{move_from_down}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_open_position}" board_fen="{fen}"
                target_square="{box_from}" />
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}" target_joint_positions="{move_from_down}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_close_position}" board_fen="{fen}"
                target_square="{box_from}" />
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}" target_joint_positions="{move_from}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_close_position}" board_fen="{fen}"
                target_square="{box_from}" />
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}" target_joint_positions="{move_to}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_close_position}" board_fen="{fen}" />
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}" target_joint_positions="{move_to_down}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_close_position}" board_fen="{fen}"
                target_square="{box_to}" />
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}" target_joint_positions="{move_to_down}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_open_position}" board_fen="{fen}"
                target_square="{box_to}" />
            <ArmActionClient player_side="{player_side}" target_joint_positions="{move_to}"
                target_hand_position="{hand_open_position}" board_fen="{fen}"
                target_square="{box_to}" />
        </Sequence>
    </BehaviorTree>
</root>
